= October 2019 — Lewica returns

For four years there was no left in the Sejm. Not a weak caucus, not a rump of
independents: none. In a country that twice elected a left-wing president and
that joined the European Union under a left-wing government, the chamber that
voted through the retirement age, the courts and the judges contained nobody
who answered to us at all.

That ended on 13 October. The list took **12.56%** and **49 deputies** — third
place, behind Law and Justice on 43.59% and 235 seats and Civic Coalition on
27.40% and 134. The opposition also took the Senate, 51 seats to 49, and two of
those are ours. It is the best thing that has happened to the Polish left since
2005, and it is not a victory. It is a return ticket.

Those 49 deputies arrive under one banner and bring every old rivalry with
them. Roughly twenty-four come from SLD, nineteen from Wiosna and six from
Razem, with trade unionists, feminists, municipal organisers and a small PPS
current threaded through the rest. SLD gives us the party machine, the legal
committee and the bank account. Wiosna gives us a public face and a modern
brand. Razem gives us a movement, a programme department and a membership that
votes on what it is told to accept. What they agreed on, in a hotel in July,
was how to win seats. They agreed nothing about what to do with them.

Mateusz Morawiecki and <span class="party-pis"><b>PiS</b></span> still command a
majority, <span class="party-pis"><b>Andrzej Duda</b></span> holds the
Presidential Palace, the Constitutional Tribunal answers to the government and
public television is a nightly broadcast against us. We must turn opposition
seats into power without being swallowed by larger parties or broken by our
own coalition.

Each month, choose one fight. Draw opportunities from party politics at home
and abroad or from government relations; keep up to three and play one. Cards
left on the table remain available, but history keeps moving.

The campaign ends with the **autumn 2027 parliamentary election**. Its verdict
will measure more than votes: what the Polish left built, changed and
survived. The first test arrives much sooner than that.

