@briefing_sld
# prose-audit: Grade A — The party's own summit is stated in results and institutions rather than nostalgia: two presidential wins, the constitution, accession, and then the specific decisions that ended it.
title: The party that wrote the constitution
subtitle: 1993–2005 — Kwaśniewski, Miller, Belka, and the fall from the top of the Republic
face-image: img/poland/events/aleksander-kwasniewski-sejm-2025.webp
new-page: true

= Briefing, part one — how high this party stood

It is worth remembering, before anyone in this club calls 12.56% a triumph or a
humiliation, what the Polish left used to be.

In 1993, four years after the fall of a system most of the country was glad to
be rid of, the post-communist Sojusz Lewicy Demokratycznej won the election with
20.41% and governed with PSL. It managed that because the anti-communist right
went to the polls in a dozen separate lists and roughly a third of all votes
elected nobody at all. The electoral threshold has decided every Polish
parliament since,
and it has never once been kind to people who could not agree in September.

Then came the part nobody predicted. In 1995 Aleksander Kwaśniewski, a minister
in the last communist government, beat Lech Wałęsa — the electrician who had
brought that system down — by 51.72% to 48.28%. In 2000 he was re-elected in the
first round with 53.90%, a thing no candidate has done before or since. A
country that despised communism twice chose a man from its apparatus, because he
was better at running the Republic than the people who had defeated it.

That left-led coalition then wrote the Constitution. The National Assembly
adopted it in April 1997, the country carried it in a referendum in May, and it
came into force on 17 October 1997. Every institution PiS is now dismantling —
the Tribunal, the judicial council, the presidential veto, the thirty-day Senate
— stands in a document our side drafted. Constitutional defence is not borrowed
politics for us. It is our own building being taken apart.

In 2001 the alliance came back with **41.04%** and 216 seats, and Leszek Miller
formed a government. It closed the accession negotiation in Copenhagen in
December 2002, won the referendum in June 2003 with **77.45%** in favour, and
took Poland into the European Union on 1 May 2004. Nothing done in this country
since 1989 has changed more lives.

And it governed, at the same time, with unemployment near twenty per cent, a
deployment to Iraq and its own occupation sector, and a finance plan whose cuts
were delivered by the party that had promised protection. Then, on 27 December
2002, Gazeta Wyborcza printed the offer: the film producer Lew Rywin asked for
seventeen and a half million dollars in exchange for an amendment to the media
law, on behalf — he said — of the group that holds power. The commission sat on live television for a year
while the country watched a governing left-wing party being asked, under oath,
who the group was.

Remember who built a career in that room. A thirty-three-year-old PiS deputy
called Zbigniew Ziobro made his name on that commission, interrogating our prime
minister in front of the whole country. He is Minister of Justice and Prosecutor
General today. The men now dismantling the courts learned their politics
prosecuting us, and they were not wrong about the thing they prosecuted.

The collapse took eighteen months. Marek Borowski left in March 2004 with a
piece of the club to found Socjaldemokracja Polska. The congress removed Miller
as chairman. He resigned as prime minister on 2 May 2004, the day after
accession, leaving on the one achievement nobody could take from him. Marek
Belka governed the remainder as a technocrat, stabilised the budget, and remains
the last left-wing prime minister this country has had. In 2005 the party took
**11.31%** and 55 seats.

The presidential race that year was worse. Włodzimierz Cimoszewicz led every
poll until a parliamentary commission produced an accusation about his asset
declaration; on 14 September 2005 he withdrew, and the stage was left to Donald
Tusk and Lech Kaczyński. Borowski took 10.33% and went home. Since that
withdrawal, no candidate of the Polish left has stood in a second round.

