@briefing_interlude
# prose-audit: Grade B — The Fourth Republic is told through the coalition arithmetic that produced it and the sting that destroyed it, with the wipe-out of both junior partners stated as the price of lending a big party its majority.
title: Two years of the Fourth Republic
subtitle: 2005–2007 — the coalition PO and PiS never formed, and the one PiS formed instead
face-image: img/poland/events/sejm-chamber.webp
new-page: true

= Briefing, part two — the right governs with the far right

In 2005 everyone in Warsaw expected a PO–PiS government. Two parties out of the
same Solidarity tradition had between them a comfortable majority and no reason
not to use it. The presidential campaign killed it. PiS spent September telling
voters about Tusk's grandfather in the Wehrmacht; Lech Kaczyński won the runoff
54.04% to 45.96%; and by November the two parties that were supposed to govern
together had learned to hate each other properly. They have not stopped since,
and the whole shape of Polish politics — every election fought as PiS against
PO, with everyone else squeezed between them — dates from that autumn.

PiS governed alone and in a minority under Kazimierz Marcinkiewicz, and then
bought a majority. A stabilisation pact in February 2006 became a formal
coalition in May with <span class="party-samoobrona">Samoobrona</span> and
<span class="party-lpr">LPR</span>, and in July Jarosław Kaczyński took the
premiership himself, with his twin brother in the Presidential Palace.

Andrzej Lepper — the agrarian populist who had made his name blocking roads with
tractors and dumping grain on railway tracks — became deputy prime minister and
minister of agriculture. Roman Giertych, of the clerical-nationalist League of
Polish Families and the All-Polish Youth, became deputy prime minister and
minister of education: reading lists purged, a campaign against so-called
homosexual propaganda in schools, and a plan to put uniformed discipline back
into every classroom. This was the first time since 1989 that the far right sat
in a Polish cabinet, and it took the right's own voters exactly two years to
decide they had seen enough.

The Fourth Republic was the programme: a new anti-corruption bureau, the CBA, a
mass lustration law the Constitutional Tribunal struck down in part — the Tribunal
still worked then — arrests staged for the evening news, and, in April 2007, the
death of Barbara Blida, a former minister who shot herself during a search of
her home by agents of the state.

It ended in a sting. Lepper was recorded around a land-rezoning bribe, dismissed
in July 2007, and the coalition disintegrated inside two months. On 7 September
the Sejm voted to dissolve itself, and in October Tusk won: PO 41.51%, PiS
32.11%, the left's Lewica i Demokraci 13.15%, PSL 8.91%.
<span class="party-samoobrona">Samoobrona</span> took 1.53% and
<span class="party-lpr">LPR</span> 1.30%. Both were annihilated, and neither has
existed in any serious form since.

That is the lesson we carry into every coalition conversation for the next eight
years: a majority assembled out of somebody else's anger can be built in three
months and destroyed in one, and the small parties that lend a big one its
numbers are the ones that get eaten.

Tusk then governed for two and a half years against a president from the
opposing camp, who vetoed what he could. Poland has spent most of its modern
history in cohabitation. It is normal here, and it is exhausting.

