@briefing_years
# prose-audit: Grade A — The liberal centre's eight years are told through the decisions that detached it from wage-earners, with Smoleńsk and its aftermath placed as the origin of the right's founding myth rather than as a disaster in isolation.
title: The warm water in the tap
subtitle: 2007–2015 — a competent centre, an aeroplane in the fog, and the country PiS inherited
face-image: img/poland/events/presidential-palace.webp
new-page: true

= Briefing, part three — the republic that managed

Donald Tusk won in 2007 with 41.5% and the largest turnout since the fall of
communism, and governed for seven years with PSL as a junior partner. He did it
by promising very little and administering competently: motorways, a European
football tournament, a banking system that survived 2008 without a single
quarter of recession. His own summary of the project was warm water in the tap
— the state as a utility that works, not a programme that changes anything.

Then, on the morning of 10 April 2010, a government Tupolev went into the fog
short of a military airfield outside Smolensk, carrying a delegation to the
seventieth anniversary of the Katyń massacres. Ninety-six people died: the
President of the Republic and the First Lady, the last president of the
government-in-exile, the chief of the general staff and the service commanders,
the president of the national bank, the head of the Solidarity strike of 1980,
and deputies of every party in the Sejm. Ours were on that aircraft too. Jerzy
Szmajdziński, the left's candidate for the presidency, died with the rest.

Under Article 131 the Marshal of the Sejm took over as acting head of state, so
the Republic was run for three months by Bronisław Komorowski, who then won the
early election against Jarosław Kaczyński by 53.01% to 46.99%. Grzegorz
Napieralski, hurriedly substituted for a dead candidate, took 13.68% for the
left — the best number we have had since, and it bought us nothing.

Scouts laid a wooden cross before the Presidential Palace five days after the
crash. When the Palace arranged in August for it to be carried to a church, a
crowd stayed on Krakowskie Przedmieście for weeks to defend it, against another
crowd that came to laugh at them; the cross finally went to St Anne's in
September, and the argument never ended. The Russian report in January 2011 and
the Polish commission's report that July both found crew error, poor training
and an airfield that should not have accepted the flight — and Russia kept the
wreckage, which guaranteed that no report would ever close the matter. Antoni
Macierewicz's people had already begun explaining the deaths as an assassination;
by 2016 that theory had a state subcommittee, exhumations carried out against the
families' wishes, and a monthly march at which a party leader spoke about the
murder of his brother. Smoleńsk is where PiS stopped being a party with a
programme and became a party with a martyrdom.

Tusk won again in 2011 anyway — the first prime minister since 1989 to be
re-elected — because a competent centre looked like shelter. Wage-earners were
told to wait, and then told to pay. In 2012 the government
raised the retirement age to sixty-seven, against roughly two million
signatures and the largest union mobilisation of the decade, and pushed it
through anyway. In 2013 and 2014 it moved the bond half of the OFE private
pension funds into the state system. Employers ran a quarter of the young
workforce on junk contracts — civil-law paper with no notice, no holiday and no
sick pay — and Platforma's
answer was that the market would eventually correct it. Nothing in eight years
made a bad job better. Everything about the arrangement said that this was as
good as Poland got.

Then the country heard how the arrangement talked about itself. In June 2014 the
tape scandal broke: Wprost published secret restaurant recordings of ministers
and central bankers over octopus and wine; the interior minister was on tape saying the Polish state
exists theoretically. Nobody resigned for months, nobody was prosecuted for the
substance, and the impression was permanent. In September Tusk left for the
presidency of the European Council. Ewa Kopacz inherited a tired cabinet
fourteen months before an election, chose continuity, defended the record she
had not written, and never established what her own government was for.

In May 2015 Bronisław Komorowski went into a re-election campaign with approval
ratings above 60% and treated it as a formality. Asked by a man who could not
live on his wages what he should do, the President suggested changing jobs or
taking out a loan. Paweł Kukiz, a rock singer running against the whole system,
took 20.8% in the first round. Andrzej Duda, a PiS backbencher most voters
could not have named in January, won the runoff 51.55% to 48.45%. In October
PiS took 37.58% and 235 seats — the first single-party majority in the history
of the Third Republic — with barely a fifth of the electorate behind it, on a
turnout of 50.9%.

A centre that governs without promising anything leaves the promise lying in
the street. In 2015 somebody picked it up.

