@briefing_tribunal
# prose-audit: Grade A — The constitutional crisis is presented as a legitimacy split with a published-judgments test rather than a partisan quarrel over one court, and public television is framed as the distribution system the Left has to work against.
title: The court that stopped being one
subtitle: November 2015 – 2018 — the Tribunal, the broadcaster and the two legal realities
face-image: img/poland/events/constitutional-tribunal.webp
new-page: true

= Briefing, part five — the constitutional crisis

It began with five judges. The outgoing Sejm elected replacements, two of them
for seats that did not fall vacant until December, which was unlawful. The
incoming Sejm annulled all five, which was also unlawful. President Duda then
declined to swear in the three lawfully elected judges and swore in the new
majority's nominees at night, in a hurry, before the Tribunal could rule on any
of it.

What made it systemic was what came next. The Tribunal ruled; the government
did not publish the ruling in the statute book; and an unpublished judgment
does not exist in Polish law. A court whose decisions can be filed in a drawer
is no longer a court. Successive laws changed its quorum, its case order and
its leadership, and by December 2016 Julia Przyłębska presided over an
institution that has not ruled against the government on anything that matters
since. In January 2016 the European Commission opened its rule-of-law
framework for the first time in the history of the Union; the Venice Commission
delivered opinion after opinion; the government carried on.

In December 2015 the majority put public television and radio directly under
the Treasury and replaced their boards. Within a year the main evening news was
an hour of government captions, and it reaches audiences we cannot buy our way
to. When the Sejm then tried to restrict journalists' access to the building in
December 2016, the opposition occupied the rostrum and the chamber for weeks,
and the budget was voted in the Column Hall, without a working vote counter, in
circumstances still disputed today. The Committee for the Defence of Democracy
brought out the largest crowds since 1989 and then destroyed its own authority
with an invoice scandal, which is what happens to movements that mistake a
crowd for an organisation.

The courts came next. In March 2016 Zbigniew Ziobro became Minister of Justice
and Prosecutor General at the same time, one man holding the ministry and the
prosecution service. In July 2017 the majority passed laws taking over the
Supreme Court and the National Council of the Judiciary; people stood outside
court buildings with candles for a week; Duda vetoed two of the three bills,
signed his own versions in December, and demonstrated that he is PiS-aligned
but not PiS-owned. The National Council is now elected by the Sejm, new
chambers sit inside the Supreme Court, and since 2018 the Court of Justice of
the European Union has been suspending pieces of the structure faster than
Warsaw dismantles them.

The result is two legal realities inside one state: judgments the government
recognises and judgments it does not, judges appointed under a body Europe does
not accept, and a Tribunal that can be pointed at any law we ever pass. Any
future majority — including one with us in it — inherits this, and cannot
repair it by simply reversing the switches.

