@briefing_streets
# prose-audit: Grade B — Four movements, one newspaper and two scandals are set out with their real outcomes, including the defeats, so mobilisation reads as a renewable but exhaustible capacity that the Left did not own.
title: Black umbrellas, occupied lecture halls, a Marshal's aeroplane
subtitle: 2016–2019 — the movements that did the opposition's work, and the arrogance that ended the term
face-image: img/poland/events/kod-constitution-2016.webp
new-page: true

= Briefing, part six — the street and the press

On 3 October 2016 — Black Monday — something like a hundred thousand people in a
hundred and fifty towns walked out in black, under umbrellas, in the rain. Three days later
the same Sejm that had sent a near-total abortion ban to committee rejected it
**352 votes to 58**, with 18 abstentions. It remains the only occasion in this
term on which the governing party publicly changed its mind because of a crowd.
It is also the outer limit of what the crowd achieved: no liberalising law
followed, the Save Women bill was thrown out in January 2018 on Black Wednesday,
and work continued on further restrictions through Black Friday in March. The
organisers are mostly women in their thirties who built local networks without
any party's help, who watched us fail to enter parliament in 2015, and who do
not assume that our support survives contact with a coalition negotiation.

In spring 2018 the universities went. Jarosław Gowin's higher-education reform,
Ustawa 2.0, concentrated power in rectors and threatened to rank provincial institutions
out of existence, and students and junior academics occupied buildings in
Warsaw, Kraków, Poznań, Wrocław and Katowice for weeks. At the same time, in a
corridor of the Sejm itself, disabled adults and their carers sat for forty
days asking for five hundred złoty a month. A government that had built its
identity on child benefit refused them, filmed them, waited them out and won.
That is the precise boundary of PiS's welfare state: generous to the family it
approves of, closed to everyone else.

In April 2019 ZNP, the teachers' union, struck for three weeks, the largest labour action of
the term, and suspended it before the school-leaving exams without a pay
agreement. They lost. The unions remember exactly which politicians appeared at
the picket and which sent a statement.

Through all of it, Gazeta Wyborcza did the reporting. It is Adam Michnik's
paper, and our relationship with it has never been friendship: in 2002 it broke
the Rywin affair against a left-wing government and helped destroy it. Since
2015 the state has withdrawn its companies' advertising, ministers and
state-owned firms have filed suits in numbers designed to cost money rather than
win, and the paper has kept publishing — including, in January 2019, Jarosław
Kaczyński's own recorded negotiations over two office towers on land owned by a
party-linked company. The lesson we take from the towers is not that the story
finished Kaczyński. It did not. The lesson is that this government is damaged by
documents, and never by adjectives.

The government changed its face rather than its programme. In December 2017
Kaczyński replaced Beata Szydło with Mateusz Morawiecki, a bank president who
speaks English at Davos, presents the same policies in the language of
investment, and has not reversed one judicial appointment. In March 2018 it
emerged that the whole outgoing cabinet had quietly paid itself bonuses;
Szydło's defence was that they had simply earned them; the government's answer
was to cut deputies' salaries by twenty per cent and make the argument about
the political class in general.

And in August, six weeks before the election, the Marshal of the Sejm resigned.
Marek Kuchciński had been flying government aircraft to Rzeszów with his
family, roughly a hundred times, on public money. Elżbieta Witek replaced him.
In September the new head of the Supreme Audit Office, Marian Banaś — PiS's own
appointee, from PiS's own finance ministry — began behaving like an auditor,
and the party discovered it had installed a hostile institution at the top of
the state.

That is the country the 49 of us walk into.

- @briefing_opposition: Continue

@briefing_left
# prose-audit: Grade A — The 2015 catastrophe is separated into its two authored failures, the candidate and the registration, with the wasted-vote arithmetic that manufactured the PiS majority stated in full.
title: What happened to us
subtitle: 2007–2016 — Miller's second term, one wasted vote in six, and 2.38%
face-image: img/poland/events/sejm-benches.webp
new-page: true

= Briefing, part four — our own ruin

After 2007 the left had thirteen per cent, four traditions and no idea what it
was for. Lewica i Demokraci — SLD with Borowski's Socjaldemokracja Polska, Unia Pracy and
the liberal Partia Demokratyczna — broke up within a year, because an alliance whose only
common position is opposition to Jarosław Kaczyński dissolves the moment
somebody has to write a manifesto. Grzegorz Napieralski's 13.68% in the 2010 presidential race
looked like a recovery, produced nothing, and was the last respectable number
this party has seen.

Leszek Miller came back as chairman in 2011 — the man who had taken us into the
European Union and out of government in the same fortnight, returning to run the
wreckage seven years later. That year SLD took 8.24% while Janusz Palikot's
brand-new anticlerical Ruch Palikota took 10.02% and finished ahead of us. Our own voters
were shopping elsewhere and had been for years, and the answer from the party
leadership was to wait for them to come back.

Then 2015, where the machine made two separate mistakes and each one alone would
have been enough.

For the presidency, Miller's SLD nominated Magdalena
Ogórek — thirty-five, telegenic, an academic with no political record, presented
to the country as a fresh face and to the party as a decision already taken.
She then ran a campaign at arm's length from the people who had nominated her,
avoided the party's own politicians, and could not answer a single question
about Iraq, about the Hausner cuts, about Rywin, because she had lived none of
it and had nothing to say about any of it. Our own deputies stopped appearing
beside her. She took **2.38%** — the worst number the Polish left has ever put
on a national ballot, lower than the party's membership could account for — and
she now reads current affairs on PiS's public television. That is what happens
when a party that cannot defend its own history hires someone to have no
history instead.

For the parliamentary election, SLD, Twój Ruch, PPS, Unia
Pracy and Zieloni went in as <span class="party-sld">Zjednoczona
Lewica</span> under Barbara Nowacka, and registered it as a coalition. A
coalition needs eight per cent. A single party's committee needs five. They took
**7.55%** and elected nobody. Razem, one year old, took **3.62%** — no seats,
but the state subvention that has funded it ever since, and votes out of the
same pool. KORWiN took 4.76% and also elected nobody.

Add it up, because everyone in this room should be able to do this arithmetic in
their sleep. Roughly **one vote in six** was cast for a list that returned not a
single deputy, and about eleven of those points were left-wing. PiS converted
**37.58%** into 235 seats and the first single-party majority in the history of
the Third Republic. Had the same five parties registered the same list under one
party's committee — the exact device we used this year — 7.55% would have
returned around thirty deputies, and there would have been no PiS majority at
all. Everything in the last four years, the Tribunal included, rests on a
registration form.

For the first time since 1989 the Sejm contained no left-wing party. In January
2016 Włodzimierz Czarzasty took over what was left: no deputies, no
parliamentary subvention, real debts and a great deal of property. For four
years we had no questions from the floor, no committee seats, no bills of our
own reaching debate, and no television. Everything the Polish left said between
2015 and this month, it said through somebody else's microphone.

- @briefing_tribunal: Continue

@briefing_opposition
# prose-audit: Grade A — The rest of the chamber is introduced as organisations with their own histories and incentives: KO's assembly out of PO and Nowoczesna, the failed 2019 grand list, the Senate pact that worked because the system punished division, PSL as the permanent hinge and Konfederacja as the pressure on PiS's right.
title: Everyone else in this opposition
subtitle: 2015–2019 — how Koalicja Obywatelska was assembled, why the Senate pact worked, and who PSL are
face-image: img/poland/events/senate-chamber.webp
new-page: true

= Briefing, part seven — the people we have to work with

We are the third-largest club in this Sejm. Being third means the other
opposition parties are not scenery; they are the parties we bargain with, lose
voters to and get blamed alongside.

Nowoczesna went up like a rocket and came down like one. Ryszard Petru, a
bank economist, founded it in May 2015 as a clean liberal alternative to a tired
Platforma: no communist past, no Tusk-era baggage, spreadsheets instead of
grievance. It took 7.60% and 28 seats, and through 2016 it polled level with PO
and occasionally ahead of it. Then, in the week the opposition was occupying the
Sejm chamber over press access, Petru flew to Madeira with a fellow deputy and
was photographed at the airport. The party never recovered; Katarzyna Lubnauer
inherited it in November 2017, deputies drifted to PO in ones and twos, and what
remained went into somebody else's list. Four years, from nothing to a third of
the liberal vote to a footnote — a warning about parties built on a mood and a
founder.

Koalicja Obywatelska is a committee, not a party. Grzegorz Schetyna, who
took over PO in January 2016, assembled it for the 2018 local elections out of
Platforma, what was left of Nowoczesna, Barbara Nowacka's Inicjatywa Polska and
Zieloni. Then, for the 2019 European election, the same people went further and
built the European Coalition — PO, PSL, ourselves, Nowoczesna and Zieloni on one
list — and lost, 38.47% to 45.38%. That defeat is why October was fought by four
separate opposition lists instead of one: the grand-coalition instinct had been
tried, in public, and had handed PiS a record score. KO holds 134 seats and
contains liberals, Christian democrats, social liberals and greens who agree
about PiS and about very little else. Its left flank competes directly with us.

The Senate pact is the one thing that worked. The Senate is elected in a
hundred single-member districts, first past the post, where two opposition
candidates in one district simply hand the seat to PiS. So KO, PSL and we
negotiated single candidates seat by seat, stood aside where somebody else was
stronger, and took the chamber **51 to 49**. Tomasz Grodzki became Marshal. Our
share is two senators: Wojciech Konieczny for PPS and Gabriela
Morawska-Stanecka, now a deputy Marshal. The lesson is exact, and it cuts both
ways — these parties can cooperate when the electoral system punishes division,
and in that negotiation we were the smallest party at the table.

PSL is the hinge, and it is not going anywhere. The peasant movement is the
oldest continuous tradition in Polish politics, older than every other party in
this chamber. It governed with the post-communist left in 1993–1997 and
2001–2003, then with Platforma from 2007 to 2015, and it survives on something
none of us have: local government, cooperatives, rural credit unions and
volunteer fire brigades in every commune in the country. It is agrarian,
Catholic and socially conservative — abortion, church, schools — and entirely
pragmatic about who it governs with. In 2019 it ran as Koalicja Polska with
Paweł Kukiz's people and took 8.55% and 30 seats under Władysław
Kosiniak-Kamysz. Arithmetic says no anti-PiS majority is possible without it.
Experience says every equality bill we ever write dies in its lap.

Konfederacja is the fifth club and PiS's right-hand problem. Free-market
radicals around Janusz Korwin-Mikke and nationalists around Krzysztof Bosak took
6.81% and 11 seats by promising to be everything PiS only pretends to be. They
will pull the government's rhetoric further right and they will describe us as
the enemy in every broadcast they can reach.

Four opposition clubs, one shared enemy, and no agreement about anything else.

