@briefing_unity
# prose-audit: Grade A — The July 2019 unity deal is presented as an arithmetic settlement with an explicit institutional asymmetry, naming what each partner brought and what none of them agreed.
title: Why there is one list at all
subtitle: 2015–2019 — Razem, Wiosna and a marriage of thresholds
face-image: img/poland/events/wlodzimierz-czarzasty-2019.webp
new-page: true

= Briefing, part eight — the alliance we actually signed

Razem was founded in May 2015 by people in their early thirties with no
post-communist inheritance to defend: housing, labour law, the quarter of the
workforce on junk contracts, public health. Adrian Zandberg walked into the one
televised debate of that campaign, argued with four party leaders, and became
the only thing the left won that year. The 3.62% brought no seats and a state
subvention, and Razem spent the next four years refusing to be absorbed by
anyone. It has circles, congresses, a binding programme and members who vote on
alliances. When Razem says no to us, it is a real no, taken by a body we do not
control. Zandberg, Magdalena Biejat, Agnieszka Dziemianowicz-Bąk, Maciej
Konieczny, Marcelina Zawisza and Paulina Matysiak now sit in our club.

Wiosna was founded on 3 February 2019 around one man. Robert Biedroń had been
the first openly gay deputy in the Sejm and then a genuinely popular mayor of
Słupsk, and he offered the left a face that carried no memory of 2004: a
secular state, climate policy, animal rights, LGBT equality, a different tone.
The launch was enormous. In the May European election Wiosna took 6.06% and
three seats — and one of them went to Leszek Miller, the man the party
establishment had removed in 2004, elected as an MEP on the list of the newest
progressive party in the country. Nothing better illustrates what the Polish
left is: too small for anybody to be finished in it. By midsummer Wiosna's
polling had been squeezed flat between PiS and the anti-PiS bloc, and standing
alone in October would have killed it.

So on 19 July 2019, Czarzasty, Biedroń and Zandberg stood together and
announced a single list; PPS joined the following day. The argument that closed
the deal was not ideological. It was the 2015 result: separately all three
would have finished under the line, together one committee clears five per
cent. On 15 August the alliance discovered it could not register a new name in
time and ran as the SLD electoral committee under the Lewica logo — so the
machine, the account and the legal identity belong to SLD, the public face
belongs to Wiosna, and the programme and the activists belong to Razem.

Nobody signed anything about the day after the election. Who leads the club,
whether SLD and Wiosna become one party, what happens to Razem's separate
registration, who is our presidential candidate, what we demand from Civic
Coalition and what we would ever accept from PSL — none of that was agreed,
because agreeing it in July would have meant not running in October.

It is now the day after the election.

