@briefing_ahead
# prose-audit: Grade A — The near horizon is stated as concrete tests with dates and numbers — the 2020 presidential benchmark, the fragility of the PiS majority and the Senate's real powers — without assuming any outcome.
title: The near horizon
subtitle: What the next months will ask of us
face-image: img/poland/events/presidential-ballot-2020.webp
new-page: true

= Briefing, part nine — what is coming

**The presidential election, spring 2020.** Andrzej Duda goes for a second term
with the incumbency, the state broadcaster and a governing party that will
spend whatever it takes. Civic Coalition will run somebody and will tell us,
loudly and often, that any left-wing candidate is a gift to PiS. We have to put
a name on the ballot anyway, because after Ogórek's 2.38% the number itself is
the verdict on this alliance. A weak first round tells every partner that unity
was a re-run of 2015 and that their exit should be planned early. A respectable
one funds the merger argument, the 2023 list and our claim to speak for the
whole left flank of the opposition. Choosing that candidate means choosing
which of the three parties gets the country's attention for six months.

The majority opposite is not one party. PiS holds 235 of 460 seats, but the
United Right is a coalition: Zbigniew Ziobro's Solidarna Polska and Jarosław
Gowin's Porozumienie each control enough deputies to end the majority on any
day either of them decides the price is right. Every vote we lose in the
chamber is worth counting anyway, because the arithmetic on the other side is
not stable.

**Our institutions are thin.** The Senate we helped win can delay a bill by
thirty days, amend it, summon ministers and hold hearings the television will
not carry — it is a stage and a brake, not a veto. The presidential veto and
the Tribunal both belong to the other side. Nothing we pass becomes law in this
term. What we can do is set the terms of argument, defend the people who are
being beaten in it, and build an organisation that survives to 2023.

SLD and Wiosna will have to decide whether they are
one party or two. Razem will not merge and should not be expected to. The union
people, the feminists, the municipal organisers and the small PPS current each
believe this club is their instrument. Money comes from dues and the seat
subvention, both finite. Time comes one fight a month, and there are always
three fights waiting.

Between here and the autumn 2027 election there are budgets every December, a
second presidential contest, a general election in 2023 and at least one moment
when somebody offers us a place in a government on terms we will not like.

The club meets in the morning.

